  "body": "It's been suggested that deploying `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` without some kind of reporting(-only) functionality makes it hard-to-impossible to deploy on entrenched setups. It's not entirely clear what the design should look like and whether it's even possible to provide enough context without violating user privacy.\r\n\r\nIn general I think we want to signal whether browsing context replacement happened (or might have happened if the user agent didn't optimize it away somehow). Here are some scenarios to consider:\r\n\r\n* NoCOOP -> COOP (this is effectively all top-level navigations to a COOP document)\r\n* COOP -> NoCOOP\r\n* COOPa -> COOPa (no report?)\r\n* COOPa -> COOPb\r\n\r\nAnother question is whether we should expose if there were multiple top-level browsing contexts in the browsing context group I think, as that would hint at more complex popup scenarios being affected.\r\n\r\nSo based on this I'd consider exposing:\r\n\r\n* URL of the document that specifies COOP (might be weird for popups, e.g., about:blank)\r\n* Whether replacement happened when navigating to that document or when navigating away (don't report user closing the tab)\r\n* Needs more thought: number of open top-level browsing contexts within the browsing context group\r\n\r\ncc @arturjanc",
